WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.089 --> 00:00:04.850
Film Courage: How does someone who hates reading
their own writing continue to write scripts?

00:00:04.850 --> 00:00:09.630
Chad Meisenheimer, Writer/Actor/Comic/Director:
I was telling my friend the other day actually

00:00:09.630 --> 00:00:16.830
about this where I only produce and write
for the stuff I want to direct or act in.

00:00:16.830 --> 00:00:18.500
I personally just want to be an actor/director.

00:00:18.500 --> 00:00:22.060
I would love to just be an actor/director.

00:00:22.060 --> 00:00:25.130
I like making my own content and telling my
own stories.

00:00:25.130 --> 00:00:29.420
That’s the main reason why I do produce
because I hate producing too.

00:00:29.420 --> 00:00:34.110
I’m good at it which is bad but I’m good
at it.

00:00:34.110 --> 00:00:39.980
Everyone wants me to produce their stuff and
I’m like “No.”

00:00:39.980 --> 00:00:42.560
My mom is also a writer.

00:00:42.560 --> 00:00:44.079
She writes really well.

00:00:44.079 --> 00:00:46.480
And I’m sitting there when I was younger
saying “I’m not going to be a writer.

00:00:46.480 --> 00:00:48.030
I’m going to be a director.”

00:00:48.030 --> 00:00:52.590
I had no aspirations of being the next Quentin
Tarantino or something like that.

00:00:52.590 --> 00:00:53.870
I just want to be a director.

00:00:53.870 --> 00:00:56.270
That’s my goal.

00:00:56.270 --> 00:01:02.091
But I just kind of sit there and am like “Ahhhh…”
and I say to my writer friends “Give me

00:01:02.091 --> 00:01:05.399
constructive feedback.”

00:01:05.399 --> 00:01:08.270
And they always laugh because I’m like “It’s
garbage.

00:01:08.270 --> 00:01:09.270
Tell me it’s garbage.”

00:01:09.270 --> 00:01:12.770
I’m not looking for compliments because
I’m the type of person, I don’t want compliments.

00:01:12.770 --> 00:01:14.720
I don’t easily give compliments out.

00:01:14.720 --> 00:01:18.609
I alway feel it’s an ulterior motive or
an agenda when people try to do that.

00:01:18.609 --> 00:01:20.100
I don’t do that.

00:01:20.100 --> 00:01:23.530
My BS meter and tolerance for it…I have
no filter.

00:01:23.530 --> 00:01:24.859
I’m just like “No.”

00:01:24.859 --> 00:01:28.570
So when I see it I’m like “Uggghhh.”

00:01:28.570 --> 00:01:31.090
My last script I was working on, I sat there
and “It’s garbage.

00:01:31.090 --> 00:01:36.060
How do they want me to make this look like
a throwback 80’s horror film but set in

00:01:36.060 --> 00:01:41.169
contemporary times?”

00:01:41.169 --> 00:01:47.049
And ironically it was me who pitched it and
I was like “How did I pull this one out

00:01:47.049 --> 00:01:48.789
of my hat?”

00:01:48.789 --> 00:01:49.899
I don’t know how I did it?

00:01:49.899 --> 00:01:51.810
I’m sitting there and spending five weeks
[typing]…the…

00:01:51.810 --> 00:02:02.029
That’s the other thing, one of my methods
of writing helps me finish writing because

00:02:02.029 --> 00:02:03.729
it would never get done.

00:02:03.729 --> 00:02:05.249
I handwrite all of my scripts.

00:02:05.249 --> 00:02:11.250
I handwrite them first before because if I
look at a blank screen, if I just see a blank

00:02:11.250 --> 00:02:12.250
screen and I’m like “Oh my gosh.

00:02:12.250 --> 00:02:13.250
My life sucks.

00:02:13.250 --> 00:02:15.269
I’m going to throw this computer out.”

00:02:15.269 --> 00:02:19.989
Another method I do to make me finish the
script, I always have an idea but I write

00:02:19.989 --> 00:02:25.180
The End and I work all the way to the beginning.

00:02:25.180 --> 00:02:30.511
The reason I started doing this is everyone
has a beginning, middle and end and they start

00:02:30.511 --> 00:02:31.900
from the beginning.

00:02:31.900 --> 00:02:33.549
I was watching the film MEMENTO.

00:02:33.549 --> 00:02:39.739
This was when I was young and I’m like “Okay,
I’m going to take the end of my script which

00:02:39.739 --> 00:02:43.939
I know how it’s going to end and I’m going
to work my way back.”

00:02:43.939 --> 00:02:47.659
Because then when I do that (my second or
third draft) I can be like “Okay I have

00:02:47.659 --> 00:02:48.659
a beginning and an end.

00:02:48.659 --> 00:02:51.960
Now I can work forward to make it tighter.”

00:02:51.960 --> 00:02:56.860
I always start with the ending first because
this character dies.

00:02:56.860 --> 00:02:57.860
No sequel!

00:02:57.860 --> 00:03:00.689
That is what I do for it.

00:03:00.689 --> 00:03:05.670
Film Courage: You have a great relationship
with your mom you said earlier and that she’s

00:03:05.670 --> 00:03:08.579
definitely honest with you about writing,
which you appreciate?

00:03:08.579 --> 00:03:09.579
Chad: Definitely.

00:03:09.579 --> 00:03:14.379
She is very on point with my writing “Okay,
this doesn’t work.

00:03:14.379 --> 00:03:15.469
This works.

00:03:15.469 --> 00:03:19.159
Try to go into more detail on this.”

00:03:19.159 --> 00:03:24.290
That’s one thing she is really into, she’s
a brilliant writer.

00:03:24.290 --> 00:03:28.000
I’m surprised she doesn’t have some successful
career as a writer?

00:03:28.000 --> 00:03:34.099
I’m just a visual person when I write (I
was explaining to her the other day) because

00:03:34.099 --> 00:03:35.799
she asked “Why don’t you have this scene?”

00:03:35.799 --> 00:03:40.700
And if I don’t see it in my head because…I
see the whole movie in my head so my goal

00:03:40.700 --> 00:03:42.370
is okay I see the whole movie in my head.

00:03:42.370 --> 00:03:46.299
I’m going to write it how I see it because
a lot of people they don’t do that.

00:03:46.299 --> 00:03:51.640
They write the story and then eventually “I
see the movie doing this and then that.”

00:03:51.640 --> 00:03:52.870
I don’t write like that.

00:03:52.870 --> 00:03:54.629
That’s also how I see scripts too.

00:03:54.629 --> 00:03:58.249
A lot of people hire me as a director sometimes.

00:03:58.249 --> 00:04:02.239
I can see the script in my head and I do exactly
what I see in my head.

00:04:02.239 --> 00:04:03.239
I see it.

00:04:03.239 --> 00:04:06.939
That’s why I like working with other writers
and working as a director-for-hire.

00:04:06.939 --> 00:04:15.980
I will always be faithful to what the writer
is writing and I know how to make it visually

00:04:15.980 --> 00:04:17.079
entertaining.

00:04:17.079 --> 00:04:19.199
A lot of people can’t do that.

00:04:19.199 --> 00:04:24.620
One of my friends is a writer and I did one
of his short films as a director a long time

00:04:24.620 --> 00:04:26.469
ago (in 2010?).

00:04:26.469 --> 00:04:29.280
He was really happy with it “This is how
I want it.”

00:04:29.280 --> 00:04:30.580
And I’m like “Cool.”

00:04:30.580 --> 00:04:35.650
Because I visualize it and can see what is
going on on the page where it’s a skill

00:04:35.650 --> 00:04:36.650
set.

00:04:36.650 --> 00:04:41.900
A lot of people who work with a director-for-hire
have issues with that sometimes where they

00:04:41.900 --> 00:04:44.240
start throwing things out of the script.

00:04:44.240 --> 00:04:45.319
The script is the blueprint.

00:04:45.319 --> 00:04:48.960
It’s the base, it’s the skeleton.

00:04:48.960 --> 00:04:50.960
Why are you getting rid of it?

00:04:50.960 --> 00:04:53.759
Just do it and come up with a creative way
to make it work.

00:04:53.759 --> 00:04:56.620
Film Courage: Do you send your mom drafts?

00:04:56.620 --> 00:05:00.919
Chad: I just sent her a draft this morning
because I wrote a new opening.

00:05:00.919 --> 00:05:02.660
Of course I didn’t turn it in soon enough.

00:05:02.660 --> 00:05:09.500
I wrote a new opening yesterday and I wanted
a better hook because it’s a horror/fantasy.

00:05:09.500 --> 00:05:14.949
The original beginning was good.

00:05:14.949 --> 00:05:22.560
But I needed something to keep it in line
when they are following this character.

00:05:22.560 --> 00:05:25.510
“Okay, something bad is going to happen.

00:05:25.510 --> 00:05:26.539
Is it during this moment?

00:05:26.539 --> 00:05:28.160
No…something bad is going to happen.”

00:05:28.160 --> 00:05:30.730
I sent it to my mom and even my writer friends.

00:05:30.730 --> 00:05:32.840
I said “Do this work?

00:05:32.840 --> 00:05:34.009
Is it too out of the blue?”

00:05:34.009 --> 00:05:38.629
They are like “No, it actually wrapped the
story up perfectly.”

00:05:38.629 --> 00:05:40.990
Which I was just like “Yes!”

00:05:40.990 --> 00:05:42.870
Usually I don’t have that happenstance moment.

00:05:42.870 --> 00:05:45.660
I’m just tired “Throw the script away.

00:05:45.660 --> 00:05:47.620
I don’t want to do this.”

00:05:47.620 --> 00:05:53.110
But it worked for some reason because I was
at Starbucks for 15 minutes and I was to the

00:05:53.110 --> 00:05:55.159
point where I wrote it out and I’m like
“Oh?

00:05:55.159 --> 00:05:56.159
I’ve got to write this fast.”

00:05:56.159 --> 00:06:01.680
I waddled home really fast and I’ve got
to write this on my computer this is a good

00:06:01.680 --> 00:06:04.130
start.

00:06:04.130 --> 00:06:08.690
She digged it and she also helps with the
spelling and grammar sometimes.

00:06:08.690 --> 00:06:12.440
Certain stuff that I miss where she’s like
“There’s a fragment sentence there and

00:06:12.440 --> 00:06:13.550
that’s not right.”

00:06:13.550 --> 00:06:18.900
And I’m like “Arrggghh…It’s a screenplay,
not a novel.”

00:06:18.900 --> 00:06:20.509
She gives good advice.

00:06:20.509 --> 00:06:21.650
She really digged the story.

00:06:21.650 --> 00:06:23.190
I was like “Oh well, okay.

00:06:23.190 --> 00:06:25.099
I guess I am doing something alright.”

00:06:25.099 --> 00:06:27.270
Film Courage: She used to write or still does?

00:06:27.270 --> 00:06:30.389
Chad: She still writes such as a lot of poetry.

00:06:30.389 --> 00:06:35.129
She has her own blog, she does a lot of arts
and crafts stuff too.

00:06:35.129 --> 00:06:37.960
But she is starting to write a lot more again.

00:06:37.960 --> 00:06:44.410
She did poetry when she was younger and stuff
that got published when she was in her teens.

00:06:44.410 --> 00:06:46.529
She a really, really good writer.

